# CSGClaw Rules

### On-demand Room Takes Precedence

- If the current message identifies an on-demand collaboration room, this section overrides global discovery, direct-agent tasks, Team skills and manual worker notification rules below, including older static instructions.
- The current Room is the entire collaboration scope. Do not list global Agents, participants, Teams or other rooms. Do not use remembered workers from another conversation. Read `csgclaw-cli room-task context --room <current_room_id>` for the only eligible roster and capabilities.
- For user work, inspect `room-task list --room <current_room_id>`, then `room-task submit --room <current_room_id> --source-message <original_user_message_id> --actor-id <requester_id> --title <goal> --body <requirements>` and `room-task plan --room <current_room_id> --task <root_task_id>`. Planning is required even with one Worker; the root is coordination, children are execution.
- Never use `task create --agent-id`, `team`, direct-agent task APIs, another execution room or a private chat for this work. If a room-task command fails or is unavailable, report the blocker; do not fall back to the old workflow or bypass runtime scope checks with raw HTTP.
- Only the recorded room-task dispatcher starts Workers. A manual mention is not a task assignment. A Worker must claim its exact dispatched child and update that child, with results delivered in the same Room.
- Do not add workers or invent assignees to satisfy a plan. Explain missing capabilities to the user. A later task must not interrupt a running task or reuse its session. Do not reset the conversation to start a task.
- The room currently permits one root; ordinary chat/status questions do not create tasks. Do not silently add work or replan. On completion, use `room-task report` for the final assessment and `room-task retry-delivery` for pending delivery, not a direct-agent task or repeated execution.

### Scope

- Use direct `csgclaw-cli` commands for routine room, participant, member, and message operations after workers already exist.
- This includes creating rooms, listing rooms or participants, listing room members, adding room members, and sending messages including structured mentions.
- Do not use this rule set to create a new worker. For new agent provisioning or hub-template creation, use the dedicated agent/template creation flow instead of bare participant-create commands.
- Do not use this rule set for multi-worker orchestration, cross-worker sequencing, or tracker-style task coordination; use `agent-teams` when team execution is intended.

### Workflow

1. Identify the exact room, participant, member, or message operation needed.
2. If room context matters, inspect it first with `room list` or `member list`, especially to determine whether the room is direct.
3. Check the current CLI surface with `csgclaw-cli <entity> -h` or `csgclaw-cli <entity> <verb> -h` before assuming flags.
4. Execute the smallest direct CLI command that completes the request.
5. Report the concrete result, such as the room ID, participant ID, member summary, or sent message result.

### Room And Participant Rules

- Use participant IDs at the CLI boundary. For the local CSGClaw manager use `manager`; use `u-manager` only for agent routes or API fields that require an agent ID.
- Run `participant list` before creating or mentioning a worker if the user may be referring to an existing participant.
- Verify room membership with `member list` when room presence matters, and after adding a member when membership is part of the task.
- A direct room cannot accept an added participant as a new member. Create a new room with the complete `--member-ids` set that includes the current DM participants and the new participant instead.
- Keep channel-specific IDs straight. In the local `csgclaw` channel use CSGClaw participant IDs; in Feishu use the configured Feishu participant IDs.

### Worker Notification Rules

- Workers are `mention_only`: they react to structured mentions, not plain-text `@name`.
- Notify workers with `csgclaw-cli message create --mention-id <participant-id>` and verify delivery with `message list`.
- Confirm that the stored message contains a structured `<at user_id="...">` tag; do not assume prose like `@worker-name` wakes the worker.
- Before first dispatch, run `participant list` to resolve the worker participant ID and `member list` to confirm the worker is in the room.

### Operating Rules

- Prefer direct `csgclaw-cli` commands over ad hoc HTTP calls.
- Keep the response focused on the concrete CLI result instead of introducing unrelated planning artifacts.
- Use `agent-teams` for multi-worker team orchestration; use manual room messaging only when direct dispatch is actually intended.

